"""Speaker notes (talk track) for the 50-slide full-project presentation. Every figure is passed in from the ledger-backed number objects."""


def notes(n, x, a):
    return [
        # A problem and context
        f"Welcome. This deck is the long version of my project: one biomass heater at an anonymized PET resin plant, an AI decision-support tool around it, and a plan for a safe pilot. Everything is synthetic. I am {a['name']}, ID {a['id']}, and I will cover the problem, the plan, the tools, the AI concepts, the chemistry, the dashboard and the pilot.",
        "The deck has seven parts. A sets the problem. B explains the plan, rules and tools. C is the data work. D is the AI and its results. E is the chemistry that checks the AI. F is the dashboard and how to use it. G is the pilot and the request. The five-slide course talk is a separate file.",
        f"Answer first. I built a read-only advisory tool and tested it on synthetic data. The simple linear model forecasts fuel as well as boosted trees, the AI option ranking is not clearly better than a rule, and at the simulated effect size the running cost exceeds the benefit. So the recommendation is to revise, approve preparation and run a read-only shadow pilot, and claim no saving.",
        "The setting is one palm-kernel-shell grate furnace that heats a thermic-fluid loop feeding a PET process. Operators set air and grate speed. Known issues are fuel use above design, operator-dependent settings, rare fuel heating-value tests and ageing fans. I only used a qualitative summary; the real drawings and screens were never read by the tool.",
        "The question is specific: in a stable, trusted window, can the tool spot avoidable fuel use and suggest a small, safe air or grate move better than a simple rule? Fuel per unit of useful heat is the measure. This is the same problem as my midterm, and I am not claiming an AI platform, only one advisory screen with a human decision.",
        f"The synthetic baseline is {n.sfc_med} kilograms of fuel per gigajoule of useful heat, with a middle band of {n.sfc_p25} to {n.sfc_p75}. Only {n.elig} percent of rows pass the data gates. The company baseline still requires validation with measured fuel mass and lab-assayed heating value, so these numbers show the method works, not what the plant does.",
        "Scope is deliberately narrow. Level one is what I built: a synthetic demonstrator. Level two is a read-only pilot that needs approvals and company data. In scope are one heater, a next-interval forecast and ranked simulated options. Out of scope is any plant control. The tool never reads real plant data, uses no network and no language model at runtime.",
        "The safety principle is the most important slide. Safety systems and the DCS keep authority. The operator decides in the existing DCS within signed limits. The advisory sits at the bottom and is read-only: no write code exists, and the safe default output is no advice with a reason code. Limits are synthetic until a signed site configuration replaces them.",
        # B plan, tools, rules
        "The project ran in eight phases from scope to a pilot package. Each phase ended with tests, a short packet describing what was built and what failed, a commit, and a human approval gate. This kept the work auditable and stopped me from running ahead of the evidence.",
        f"This table shows what each phase delivered and the evidence. Phase one produced {n.rows_total} rows of synthetic data. Phase three chose the linear model. Phase four produced {n.adv} advised rows out of {n.test_rows} replayed. Phase eight added the pilot package. The last full build ran {n.tests_passed} automated tests.",
        "These working rules shaped every decision. Synthetic data only, and every number labelled with its provenance. No plant control and a fail-safe no-advice output. No leakage: chronological, lot-aware splits and trailing windows only. Thermic fluid properties from a table, never water. No network and no real company names or logos in outputs.",
        "These are the data and machine-learning tools, with why, how and what. Python with pandas and numpy for transparency; scikit-learn for linear and tree models; scipy for statistics; joblib for saved models; pydantic for schema checks; YAML for configuration; pytest for repeatable evidence. Versions are read from the environment, not typed.",
        "These are the application and operations tools. Streamlit and Plotly make the local dashboard; SQLite holds the logs; ReportLab, python-pptx and openpyxl build documents; Docker packages the tool with no outbound network; Git records each phase; and an AI coding assistant sped up the work under my review. The Docker image has not been built yet because a build needs network access.",
        f"AI help was allowed, and I remain responsible for every claim. A separate critic agent reviewed the models and gave {x.n_findings} findings, of which {x.n_genuine} were real errors. The clearest was a weak reference that made the linear model look {n.L_vs_P} percent better, when the fair gain is {n.L_vs_P2} percent. Tests and a source map check the rest.",
        # C data
        "Why synthetic? I may not read real plant data with this tool, and synthetic data gives known truth, so I can test whether the method works and whether the guardrails behave. What it cannot show is real savings or real confounding. A read-only pilot with measured fuel mass and assayed heating value is the only route to a savings claim.",
        "The generator builds physics first: heat demand, fuel lots with moisture and heating value, a latent efficiency that depends on air, grate, fouling and fans, then fuel from heat divided by efficiency and heating value. Measured values add noise, bias, lag, faults and gaps. Three seeds separate the main run, a stress run and a site-style run.",
        f"The data dictionary documents {x.n_dict} columns with group, unit, role and provenance. About {x.n_site_cols} could be supplied by a site and {x.n_critical} of those are critical. Truth and label columns exist only for evaluation and are never model inputs. Tag names are synthetic aliases, not real tags.",
        f"Cleaning turns {x.n_raw} raw rows into {x.n_clean} clean rows and rejects {x.n_rej}. It checks schema and units, duplicates and gaps, frozen sensors, totalizer resets, heat and fuel plausibility, and aligns the fuel-to-heat lag of {x.lag} steps. Raw data is never modified, nothing is interpolated, and missing heating value stays missing.",
        "Efficiency and fuel per unit of heat come from a heat balance. Useful heat is HTF mass flow times heat capacity times temperature rise. Fuel heat is fuel rate times heating value. Efficiency is their ratio and SFC follows from it. The worked example on the right comes from the specification, and water heat capacity is never used for the thermic fluid.",
        "Unit slips are a classic source of bad numbers, so conversions live in one tested module: millimetres of water column to pascals, Mkcal per hour to megawatts, kilograms per second to kilograms per hour and others. A round trip on a site-style export is exact, and the cleaning step flags reported heat that looks like a unit slip.",
        f"Data gates decide whether a row may receive advice. Layer one checks data validity and steadiness; layer two checks limits such as draft, oxygen, CO, trips and derates; then the optimizer runs. On the replayed period {n.adv} rows got advice, {n.noadv} got no advice and {n.diag} were diagnostic only. Only {n.elig} percent of rows are eligible, and that is intended.",
        f"The energy baseline models SFC as a function of load and moisture, fitted on eligible windows with lot-cluster bootstrap intervals. It explains most of the variation with an R squared of {x.r2} and a test error of {x.mae_base} kilograms per gigajoule. It shows that moisture and load matter; it is not a plant claim. A simple rule alert is the comparison method.",
        # D AI
        "These are the AI concepts used, in plain words. Regression forecasts fuel. Honest baselines keep models honest. Boosted trees were tested but not chosen. Ridge regression with bootstrap gives the action effect and its uncertainty. Conformal prediction gives intervals. Out-of-distribution detection refuses unfamiliar states. Physics models explain why. Guardrails and a human in the loop keep it safe.",
        "The forecast task predicts the mean fuel rate over the next fifteen minutes using only what is known now: demand, fuel lags, lot moisture and heating value from the lab, oxygen, CO, settings, temperatures, draft and timing features. Efficiency, SFC, fuel at the current time and any truth or label column are never inputs.",
        "Leakage is the classic way to fool yourself, so splits are chronological and lot-aware, with a few rows purged at each boundary. Four automatic tests check that a shuffled target has no skill, that a deliberately added future feature is caught, that features do not change when later rows are removed, and that forbidden columns never reach the model.",
        "Seven candidates were compared: persistence, repeating the current feed, a physics formula, the two-variable regression, a linear model on all features, and boosted trees with and without settings. The rule was fixed before training: the simplest safe candidate wins unless a more complex one is at least ten percent better.",
        f"On later fuel lots, the linear model has a mean error of {n.mae_L} kilograms per hour and boosted trees {n.mae_C0}. The two-variable regression is {n.mae_B}. The linear model is {n.L_vs_B} percent better than the regression and {n.L_vs_P2} percent better than repeating the current feed. The gain comes from using more features, not from complexity.",
        f"This is the AI error I want to show. My first comparison used the last fuel value as the reference, and the linear model looked {n.L_vs_P} percent better. A critic review noted the models also see the current feed. With the fair reference the gain is {n.L_vs_P2} percent. I fixed the reference, moved selection to validation rows and strengthened the leakage test.",
        f"Uncertainty uses split conformal prediction: take the ninetieth percentile of absolute errors on validation rows the model did not train on and use it as the half-width. It needs no distribution assumption. Test coverage for the chosen model is {n.cov_L} percent against a target of ninety. Its limit is one half-width everywhere.",
        f"The optimizer scores {x.n_cand} small moves of the forward fan and grate with one objective: the change in SFC plus penalties for fan power, move size and uncertainty. Moves are limited to {x.step} percent per axis, and options that break the oxygen floor, CO, draft, range or distribution checks are dropped. The effect model is associational, not proven causal.",
        f"Guardrails make no advice the default. There are {x.n_codes} reason codes covering data validity, safety limits, missing information, the optimizer and monitor-forced states. Advice appears only if everything passes and the benefit is clear. If CO is missing the output is diagnostic only. Every output says human review required.",
        f"Comparing option ranking with the rule on the same rows: the AI finds the exact best move on {n.ai_hit} percent of actionable rows against {n.rule_hit} for the rule with a default move, but its false-alert rate is {n.ai_fa} percent against {n.rule_fa}. The AI is not clearly better, so the pilot baseline is the rule and the regression.",
        # E chemistry
        f"Chemistry gives an independent check. Oxygen needed comes from carbon, hydrogen, sulphur and oxygen in the fuel; stoichiometric air follows; and excess air is twenty-one over twenty-one minus the measured oxygen. For the example row, oxygen of {x.o2_now} percent means {x.lam} times the stoichiometric air of {x.stoich} kilograms per kilogram of fuel. The fuel analysis is assumed.",
        f"The indirect method adds up the losses. For the example row dry flue gas loses {x.l_dry} percent of the fuel heat, water vapour {x.l_vap}, CO {x.l_co}, unburnt carbon {x.l_unb} and radiation {x.l_rad}, an assumption. That gives {x.eta_chem} percent, against {x.eta_hb} percent from the HTF heat balance. The gap is a closure check because the synthetic efficiency is a generator assumption.",
        f"Cutting air lowers the stack loss, but too little air raises CO and unburnt carbon. The best allowed oxygen here is {x.o2_opt} percent, which is the lower safety bound, so the oxygen floor and CO limit, not the model, decide how far to go. The chemistry change of {x.d_chem} points has the same sign as the ridge model's {x.d_ridge}, a consistency check on the AI.",
        f"The mass balance closes to {x.close} percent, and the heat balance shows fuel heat, losses and useful heat. The largest levers are air, worth {x.lev_o2} points, and stack temperature, worth {x.lev_stack} points per ten kelvin of cleaning. The best reachable efficiency inside the limits is {x.eta_best} percent against {x.eta_chem} now. Stack and moisture are not real-time controls.",
        # F dashboard
        f"The dashboard has {x.n_pages} pages along the top: Live, Combustion, Optimize, Advice, What-if, Decisions and Value. Each page carries the red synthetic banner, a time zone label, status badges and a provenance note under every chart. This table says what you see on each page and what to use it for.",
        "To start the dashboard, open PowerShell in the project folder, run run_dashboard.ps1, and open localhost port 8501 in a browser on the same computer. It listens on this computer only and needs no internet. First load takes a few seconds, then data are cached. Stop it with Control C. Other commands rebuild results, run the tests and retake screenshots.",
        "These are all the inputs. In the sidebar choose the data source and the date range. On Live and Optimize use the replay slider, step buttons and Play. On Advice pick the row filter and timestamp. On What-if enter fan and grate targets inside the bounds; outside gives an error and no result. On Decisions log a role, a decision, a reason and a short note. On Value edit the assumptions.",
        "On the Live page read the tiles first: HTF outlet, fuel, SFC, useful heat, efficiency and the advisory gate. Badges tell you how far to trust each number. Efficiency shows an interval when the fuel lot has no lab heating value. The SFC chart shows a baseline band that is a reference, not a target.",
        "The Combustion page shows lab moisture and heating value per lot with gaps left visible, oxygen and CO against the synthetic floor and limit, the fan and grate commands, temperatures, draft, ash, economizer pressure drop and the fault table. Remember that fan values are commands, not airflow, and that rows without CO can only be diagnostic.",
        f"The first half of the Optimize page gives the status: efficiency now {x.eta_chem} percent, best reachable {x.eta_best} percent, room to improve {x.gain} points and the gate. Then the list of what to change now, air first, and a table of every operating parameter with target, status and action. Take one bounded step, then check CO and draft.",
        "The second half of the Optimize page shows the levers chart, the heat balance waterfall, the predicted effect of forward-fan moves with their checks, the mass balance and a six-hour trend. A move is a candidate only if every constraint passes. The unaccounted heat is what the chemistry cannot explain.",
        "On the Advice page read the gate first. The default is a large red no advice with reason codes. On advice you see current against proposed settings, the predicted change with its ninety percent interval and a seven-line checklist. Below is the chemistry view. A person always reviews, and nothing here is an instruction.",
        "Three more pages. What-if lets you try a fan and grate target inside the bounds and see the predicted effect and checks. Decisions records the human decision with a role ID, a reason and a note, in a log with a hash chain. Value shows the simulated index and the money sensitivity on assumed inputs, always with the message that no savings are verified.",
        # G pilot
        "People stay in charge. The shift supervisor makes and logs each decision, the safety lead re-checks every advisory, the process engineer judges false alerts, the energy manager measures the effect, the data scientist runs the monitor and the sponsor decides at week six. The main risk is following a wrong suggestion; the control is advisory-only output, no advice by default and untouched safety systems.",
        f"The pilot runs {n.weeks} weeks, read-only. Weeks one and two are approvals and data, weeks three and four are shadow with blind review, week five is supervised advice only if authorised, and week six is measurement and the decision. There are {n.n_targets} countable targets and {n.n_stops} stop conditions, quoted verbatim from the pilot specification.",
        f"Computing for the whole pilot needs about {n.kwh6_base} kilowatt-hours, from an assumed 60 watts times measured run times, which is negligible. The simulated fuel effect after an assumed realisation share is {n.sim_base} percent, below the {n.be_base} percent needed to cover running cost. Money figures are fictional ranges, and I make no carbon claim because no lifecycle boundary is defined.",
        f"The pilot package turns the demonstrator into something a site can try: read-only adapters with a tag map, a site configuration that refuses advisory mode until limits and approvals are complete, retraining with a strict promotion rule, a monitor, a tamper-evident audit log and measurement and verification. A known minus three percent effect is recovered as {x.inj} percent; with no effect the estimate is {x.aa} percent.",
        f"My request is a {n.weeks}-week read-only pilot on one heater, with a fictional cost range of {n.one_opt} to {n.one_pess} million baht one-time and the project sponsor as owner. Go means all {n.n_targets} targets met on measured data and none of the {n.n_stops} stop conditions. The recommendation is to revise before any go. Thank you; I welcome questions.",
    ]
